{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We ended the last lesson with a precise requirement: we need to select a stored value using a number the program can compute, rather than a name a human typed. Let us design something that does that."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "One name, many slots"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Start by refusing to invent 108 names. We will use exactly one — `ages` — for the entire collection. That immediately raises the question the whole design hinges on: if every value shares a name, how do we say which one we mean?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We number them. The name gets you to the collection; a number gets you to one slot inside it."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/5b024bbf-7361-4bf0-9d3b-f2962c15d049.svg",
      "alt": "One block of eight adjacent slots under a single name, numbered zero through seven",
      "caption": "One name for the whole block, one number to pick a slot",
      "width": 900,
      "height": 300,
      "scene": {
