    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That number is called the **index**, and because it is an ordinary integer, your program can compute it. `ages[i]` works for any `i` — including an `i` that a loop is changing on every pass. That is the whole breakthrough."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "let total = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i < 108; i++) {\n  total += ages[i];\n}\nconst average = total / 108;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Five lines, and they would work just as well for 108 students as for 10,000. Compare that to the 108-name sum from the last lesson."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why the slots sit next to each other"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There is a second decision hiding in that picture, and it is easy to miss: the slots are drawn **adjacent**, in one unbroken run of memory. That is not decoration."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If the values were scattered around memory, the program would need a lookup table mapping each index to wherever that value ended up — and consulting that table would cost time that grows with the size of the collection. By insisting the slots are consecutive, the position of any slot becomes something you can calculate from the position of the first one. We will do that calculation in a couple of lessons."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why counting starts at zero"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Almost every language numbers the first slot **0**, not 1, and it trips up nearly everyone at first. The reason becomes obvious once you think of the index as a **distance** rather than a position."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The index answers: *how far from the start is this slot?* The first slot is where the block begins, so it is zero slots away. The second is one slot along. Under that reading, `ages[0]` is not an off-by-one quirk — it is the only number that makes sense."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "`ages[0]` — zero slots from the start, so the first value",
        "`ages[3]` — three slots along, so the fourth value",
        "`ages[107]` — the last slot in a 108-slot block"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is also why the highest valid index is always one less than the length. A block of 108 slots runs from 0 to 107, and asking for `ages[108]` asks for a slot that is 108 steps from the start — one step past the end of what you reserved."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "What we have just described *is* an array. The next lesson looks at what it lets you do, and what each of those things costs."
    }
